<p id=mwAeA>In January 2007, Wikipedia first became one of the ten <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_most-visited_websites title="List of most-visited websites" id=mwAeE>most popular websites</a> in the United States, according to <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comscore title=Comscore id=mwAeI>Comscore</a> Networks.<sup about=#mwt237 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Comscore-2007_73-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Comscore-2007"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Comscore-2007-73"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Comscore-2007-73 id=mwAeM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAeQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAeU>[</span>66<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAeY>]</span></span></a></sup> With 42.9<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwAec>&nbsp;</span>million unique visitors, it was ranked ninth, surpassing <i id=mwAeg><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times title="The New York Times" id=mwAek>The New York Times</a></i> (No. 10) and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc. title="Apple Inc." id=mwAeo>Apple</a> (No. 11).<sup about=#mwt238 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Comscore-2007_73-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Comscore-2007"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Comscore-2007-73 id=mwAes><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAew><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAe0>[</span>66<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAe4>]</span></span></a></sup> This marked a significant increase over January 2006, when Wikipedia ranked 33rd, with around 18.3<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwAe8>&nbsp;</span>million unique visitors.<sup about=#mwt241 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-74 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-74"}}'><a href=#cite_note-74 id=mwAfA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAfE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAfI>[</span>67<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAfM>]</span></span></a></sup> In 2014, it received 8<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwAfQ>&nbsp;</span>billion page views every month.<sup about=#mwt244 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-75 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-75"}}'><a href=#cite_note-75 id=mwAfU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAfY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAfc>[</span>68<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAfg>]</span></span></a></sup> On February 9, 2014, <i id=mwAfk>The New York Times</i> reported that Wikipedia had 18<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwAfo>&nbsp;</span>billion <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pageview title=Pageview id=mwAfs>page views</a> and nearly 500<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwAfw>&nbsp;</span>million <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unique_user#Unique_visitor title="Unique user" id=mwAf0>unique visitors</a> a month, "according to the ratings firm comScore".<sup about=#mwt247 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-small_screen_76-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"small screen"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-small_screen-76"}}'><a href=#cite_note-small_screen-76 id=mwAf4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAf8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAgA>[</span>69<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAgE>]</span></span></a></sup> <span about=#mwt234 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwAgI data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"as of","href":"./Template:As_of"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2023"},"2":{"wt":"March"}},"i":0}}]}'>As of March</span><span typeof=mw:Entity about=#mwt234>&nbsp;</span><span about=#mwt234>2023</span><sup class="plainlinks noexcerpt noprint asof-tag update" style=display:none about=#mwt234></sup><span typeof=mw:Nowiki about=#mwt234></span><link rel=mw:PageProp/Category href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements_from_March_2023 about=#mwt234><link rel=mw:PageProp/Category href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:All_articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements about=#mwt234 id=mwAgM>, it ranked sixth in popularity, according to <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Similarweb title=Similarweb id=mwAgQ>Similarweb</a>.<sup about=#mwt250 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-77 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-77"}}'><a href=#cite_note-77 id=mwAgU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAgY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAgc>[</span>70<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAgg>]</span></span></a></sup> Jeff Loveland and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Reagle title="Joseph Reagle" id=mwAgk>Joseph Reagle</a> argue that, in process, Wikipedia follows a long tradition of historical encyclopedias that have accumulated improvements piecemeal through "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stigmergy title=Stigmergy id=mwAgo>stigmergic</a> accumulation".<sup about=#mwt253 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-sagepub_WP_and_encyclopedic_production_1_78-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"sagepub WP and encyclopedic production 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-sagepub_WP_and_encyclopedic_production_1-78"}}'><a href=#cite_note-sagepub_WP_and_encyclopedic_production_1-78 id=mwAgs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAgw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAg0>[</span>71<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAg4>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt256 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-theatlantic_WP_actually_a_reversion_1_79-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"theatlantic WP actually a reversion 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-theatlantic_WP_actually_a_reversion_1-79"}}'><a href=#cite_note-theatlantic_WP_actually_a_reversion_1-79 id=mwAg8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAhA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAhE>[</span>72<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAhI>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAhM><span class=anchor id=BlackoutProtest about=#mwt257 typeof=mw:Transclusion data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"anchor","href":"./Template:Anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"BlackoutProtest"}},"i":0}}]}'></span>
On January 18, 2012, the English Wikipedia participated in a series of coordinated protests against two proposed laws in the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Congress title="United States Congress" id=mwAhQ>United States Congress</a>—the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stop_Online_Piracy_Act title="Stop Online Piracy Act" id=mwAhU>Stop Online Piracy Act</a> (SOPA) and the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PROTECT_IP_Act title="PROTECT IP Act" id=mwAhY>PROTECT IP Act</a> (PIPA)—by <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protests_against_SOPA_and_PIPA title="Protests against SOPA and PIPA" id=mwAhc>blacking out its pages for 24 hours</a>.<sup about=#mwt260 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-LA_Times_Jan_19_80-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"LA Times Jan 19"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-LA_Times_Jan_19-80"}}'><a href=#cite_note-LA_Times_Jan_19-80 id=mwAhg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAhk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAho>[</span>73<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAhs>]</span></span></a></sup> More than 162<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwAhw>&nbsp;</span>million people viewed the blackout explanation page that temporarily replaced its content.<sup about=#mwt263 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-BBC_WP_blackout_protest_1_81-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"BBC WP blackout protest 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-BBC_WP_blackout_protest_1-81"}}'><a href=#cite_note-BBC_WP_blackout_protest_1-81 id=mwAh0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAh4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAh8>[</span>74<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAiA>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt266 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-82 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-82"}}'><a href=#cite_note-82 id=mwAiE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAiI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAiM>[</span>75<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAiQ>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAiU>In January 2013, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/274301_Wikipedia title="274301 Wikipedia" id=mwAiY>274301 Wikipedia</a>, an <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asteroid title=Asteroid id=mwAic>asteroid</a>, was named after Wikipedia;<sup about=#mwt269 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-83 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-83"}}'><a href=#cite_note-83 id=mwAig><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAik><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAio>[</span>76<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAis>]</span></span></a></sup> in October 2014, Wikipedia was honored with the <i id=mwAiw><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia_Monument title="Wikipedia Monument" id=mwAi0>Wikipedia Monument</a></i>;<sup about=#mwt272 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-84 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-84"}}'><a href=#cite_note-84 id=mwAi4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAi8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAjA>[</span>77<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAjE>]</span></span></a></sup> and, in July 2015, 106 of the 7,473 700-page volumes of Wikipedia became available as <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Print_Wikipedia title="Print Wikipedia" id=mwAjI>Print Wikipedia</a>.<sup about=#mwt275 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-85 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-85"}}'><a href=#cite_note-85 id=mwAjM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAjQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAjU>[</span>78<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAjY>]</span></span></a></sup> In April 2019, an Israeli <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_lander title="Lunar lander" id=mwAjc>lunar lander</a>, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beresheet title=Beresheet id=mwAjg>Beresheet</a>, crash landed on the surface of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon title=Moon id=mwAjk>Moon</a> carrying a copy of nearly all of the English Wikipedia engraved on thin nickel plates; experts say the plates likely survived the crash.<sup about=#mwt278 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-WRD-20190805_86-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WRD-20190805"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-WRD-20190805-86"}}'><a href=#cite_note-WRD-20190805-86 id=mwAjo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAjs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAjw>[</span>79<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAj0>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt281 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-VOX-20190806_87-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"VOX-20190806"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-VOX-20190806-87"}}'><a href=#cite_note-VOX-20190806-87 id=mwAj4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAj8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAkA>[</span>80<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAkE>]</span></span></a></sup> In June 2019, scientists reported that all 16<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwAkI>&nbsp;</span>GB of article text from the English Wikipedia had been encoded into <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synthetic_genomics title="Synthetic genomics" id=mwAkM>synthetic DNA</a>.<sup about=#mwt284 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-CNET-20190629_88-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"CNET-20190629"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-CNET-20190629-88"}}'><a href=#cite_note-CNET-20190629-88 id=mwAkQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAkU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAkY>[</span>81<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAkc>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAkg>On January 18, 2023, Wikipedia debuted a new website redesign, called "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vector_2022 title="Vector 2022" class=mw-redirect id=mwAkk>Vector 2022</a>".<sup about=#mwt287 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Pearl-2023_89-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Pearl-2023"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Pearl-2023-89"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Pearl-2023-89 id=mwAko><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAks><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAkw>[</span>82<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAk0>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt290 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Tech_Desk-2023_90-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tech Desk-2023"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Tech_Desk-2023-90"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Tech_Desk-2023-90 id=mwAk4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAk8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAlA>[</span>83<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAlE>]</span></span></a></sup> It featured a redesigned <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menu_bar title="Menu bar" id=mwAlI>menu bar</a>, moving the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_of_contents title="Table of contents" id=mwAlM>table of contents</a> to the left as a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sidebar_(computing) title="Sidebar (computing)" id=mwAlQ>sidebar</a>, and numerous changes in the locations of buttons like the language selection tool.<sup about=#mwt291 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Tech_Desk-2023_90-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tech Desk-2023"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Tech_Desk-2023-90 id=mwAlU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAlY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAlc>[</span>83<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAlg>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt294 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-91 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-91"}}'><a href=#cite_note-91 id=mwAlk><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAlo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAls>[</span>84<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAlw>]</span></span></a></sup> The update initially received backlash, most notably when editors of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swahili_Wikipedia title="Swahili Wikipedia" id=mwAl0>Swahili Wikipedia</a> unanimously voted to revert the changes.<sup about=#mwt295 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Pearl-2023_89-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Pearl-2023"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Pearl-2023-89 id=mwAl4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAl8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAmA>[</span>82<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAmE>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt298 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-92 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-92"}}'><a href=#cite_note-92 id=mwAmI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAmM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAmQ>[</span>85<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAmU>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAmY>Both Sanger and Wales gave public interviews in late 2025 about their reflections about the status and state of Wikipedia, leading up to its 25 years of operation on January 15, 2026; Wales appeared on the PBS television news show GZERO World interviewed by <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ian_Bremmer title="Ian Bremmer" id=mwAmc>Ian Bremmer</a><sup about=#mwt299 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-93 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-93"}}'><a href=#cite_note-93 id=mwAmg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAmk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAmo>[</span>86<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAms>]</span></span></a></sup> and Sanger has appeared on the Fox News Channel, interviewed by <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashley_Rindsberg title="Ashley Rindsberg" id=mwAmw>Ashley Rindsberg</a>.<sup about=#mwt300 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-94 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-94"}}'><a href=#cite_note-94 id=mwAm0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAm4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAm8>[</span>87<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAnA>]</span></span></a></sup> Wales' book <i id=mwAnE><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seven_Rules_of_Trust title="The Seven Rules of Trust" id=mwAnI>The Seven Rules of Trust</a></i> was published in October 2025 by <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penguin_Random_House title="Penguin Random House" id=mwAnM>Penguin Random House</a>. It was described by the publisher as a "sweeping reflection on the global crisis of credibility and knowledge" with the book examining the "rules of trust" that enabled the growth and success of Wikipedia.<sup about=#mwt303 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Penguin_95-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Penguin"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Penguin-95"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Penguin-95 id=mwAnQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAnU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAnY>[</span>88<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAnc>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section><section data-mw-section-id=6 id=mwAng aria-labelledby=Impacts_of_generative_AI_on_Wikipedia_views><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=Impacts_of_generative_AI_on_Wikipedia_views>Impacts of generative AI on Wikipedia views</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt304 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwAnk data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Artificial intelligence in Wikimedia projects"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237 about=#mwt305 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><div role=note class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about=#mwt304 id=mwAno>Further information: <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence_in_Wikimedia_projects title="Artificial intelligence in Wikimedia projects">Artificial intelligence in Wikimedia projects</a></div>
<p id=mwAns>Since January 2024, the Wikimedia Foundation has reported a roughly 50 percent increase in bandwidth use from downloads of multimedia content across its projects. According to the foundation, this growth is largely attributed to automated programs, or "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping title="Web scraping" id=mwAnw>scraper</a>" bots, that collect large volumes of data from Wikimedia sites for use in training large language models and related applications.<sup about=#mwt308 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-96 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-96"}}'><a href=#cite_note-96 id=mwAn0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAn4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAn8>[</span>89<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAoA>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAoE>In October 2025, the Wikimedia Foundation reported an estimated 8 percent decline in <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_traffic title="Internet traffic" id=mwAoI>traffic</a> as compared to the same month in 2024 in human page views. They speculated that it reflects the growing use of <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generative_AI title="Generative AI" id=mwAoM>generative AI</a> and social media for finding information.<sup about=#mwt311 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-97 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-97"}}'><a href=#cite_note-97 id=mwAoQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAoU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAoY>[</span>90<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAoc>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt314 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-98 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-98"}}'><a href=#cite_note-98 id=mwAog><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAok><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAoo>[</span>91<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAos>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section></section><section data-mw-section-id=7 id=mwAow aria-labelledby=Collaborative_editing><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=Collaborative_editing>Collaborative editing</h2></div>
<figure class=mw-default-size typeof=mw:File/Thumb id=mwAo0><a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:History_Comparison_Example_(Vector).png class=mw-file-description id=mwAo4><img resource=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:History_Comparison_Example_(Vector).png src="data:image/png;base64," decoding=async loading=lazy data-file-width=1225 data-file-height=768 data-file-type=bitmap height=157 width=250 class=mw-file-element id=mwAo8></a><figcaption id=mwApA>Differences between versions of an article are highlighted.</figcaption></figure>
<section data-mw-section-id=8 id=mwApE aria-labelledby=Restrictions><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=Restrictions>Restrictions</h3></div>
<p id=mwApI>Due to Wikipedia's increasing popularity, some editions, including the English version, have introduced editing restrictions for certain cases. For instance, on the English Wikipedia and some other language editions, only users with 10 edits that have an account that is four days old may create a new article.<sup about=#mwt321 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-99 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-99"}}'><a href=#cite_note-99 id=mwApM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwApQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwApU>[</span>92<span class=cite-bracket id=mwApY>]</span></span></a></sup> On the English Wikipedia, among others, particularly controversial, sensitive, or vandalism-prone pages have been protected to varying degrees.<sup about=#mwt324 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-100 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-100"}}'><a href=#cite_note-100 id=mwApc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwApg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwApk>[</span>93<span class=cite-bracket id=mwApo>]</span></span></a></sup> A frequently vandalized article can be "semi-protected" or "extended confirmed protected", meaning that only "autoconfirmed" or "extended confirmed" editors can modify it.<sup about=#mwt315 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202212–13_101-0 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202212–13"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202212–13-101"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"pages":{"wt":"12–13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202212%E2%80%9313-101 id=mwAps><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwApw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAp0>[</span>94<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAp4>]</span></span></a></sup> A particularly contentious article may be locked so that only <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia_administrators title="Wikipedia administrators" id=mwAp8>administrators</a> can make changes.<sup about=#mwt325 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-102 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-102"}}'><a href=#cite_note-102 id=mwAqA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAqE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAqI>[</span>95<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAqM>]</span></span></a></sup> A 2021 article in the <i id=mwAqQ><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_Journalism_Review title="Columbia Journalism Review" id=mwAqU>Columbia Journalism Review</a></i> identified Wikipedia's page-protection policies as "perhaps the most important" means at its disposal to "regulate its market of ideas".<sup about=#mwt328 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-103 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-103"}}'><a href=#cite_note-103 id=mwAqY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAqc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAqg>[</span>96<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAqk>]</span></span></a></sup> Wikipedia has delegated some functions to <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligent_agent title="Intelligent agent" id=mwAqo>bots</a>. Such <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_by_algorithm title="Government by algorithm" id=mwAqs>algorithmic governance</a> has an ease of implementation and scaling, though the automated rejection of edits may have contributed to a downturn in active Wikipedia editors.<sup about=#mwt331 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Ren-2024_104-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Ren-2024"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Ren-2024-104"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Ren-2024-104 id=mwAqw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAq0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAq4>[</span>97<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAq8>]</span></span></a></sup> Bots must be approved by the community before their tasks are implemented.<sup about=#mwt318 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202236_105-0 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202236"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202236-105"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"page":{"wt":"36"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202236-105 id=mwArA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwArE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwArI>[</span>98<span class=cite-bracket id=mwArM>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwArQ>In certain cases, all editors are allowed to submit modifications, but review is required for some editors, depending on certain conditions. For example, the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_Wikipedia title="German Wikipedia" id=mwArU>German Wikipedia</a> maintains "stable versions" of articles which have passed certain reviews.<sup about=#mwt334 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-WP_some_sites_stable_versions_1_106-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WP some sites stable versions 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-WP_some_sites_stable_versions_1-106"}}'><a href=#cite_note-WP_some_sites_stable_versions_1-106 id=mwArY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwArc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwArg>[</span>99<span class=cite-bracket id=mwArk>]</span></span></a></sup> Following protracted trials and community discussion, the English Wikipedia introduced the "pending changes" system in December 2012.<sup about=#mwt337 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-BInsider_pending_changes_intro_1_107-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"BInsider pending changes intro 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-BInsider_pending_changes_intro_1-107"}}'><a href=#cite_note-BInsider_pending_changes_intro_1-107 id=mwAro><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwArs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwArw>[</span>100<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAr0>]</span></span></a></sup> Under this system, new and unregistered users' edits to certain controversial or vandalism-prone articles are reviewed by established users before they are published.<sup about=#mwt340 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-108 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-108"}}'><a href=#cite_note-108 id=mwAr4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAr8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAsA>[</span>101<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAsE>]</span></span></a></sup> However, restrictions on editing may reduce editor engagement as well as efforts to diversify the editing community.<sup about=#mwt343 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-109 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-109"}}'><a href=#cite_note-109 id=mwAsI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAsM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAsQ>[</span>102<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAsU>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAsY>Articles related to the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Israeli%E2%80%93Palestinian_conflict title="Israeli–Palestinian conflict" id=mwAsc>Israeli–Palestinian conflict</a> are placed under extended-confirmed protection.<sup about=#mwt346 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Harrison-2023_110-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Harrison-2023"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Harrison-2023-110"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Harrison-2023-110 id=mwAsg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAsk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAso>[</span>103<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAss>]</span></span></a></sup> Editors also can make only one revert per day across the entire field and can be banned from editing related articles. These restrictions were introduced in 2008.<sup about=#mwt349 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Benjakob-2020a_111-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Benjakob-2020a"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Benjakob-2020a-111"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Benjakob-2020a-111 id=mwAsw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAs0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAs4>[</span>104<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAs8>]</span></span></a></sup> In January 2025, the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbitration_Committee_(Wikipedia) title="Arbitration Committee (Wikipedia)" class=mw-redirect id=mwAtA>Arbitration Committee</a> introduced the "balanced editing restriction", which requires sanctioned users to devote only a third of their edits to articles related to the Israeli–Palestinian conflict even when no misconduct rules have been violated.<sup about=#mwt352 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Harrison-2025_112-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Harrison-2025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Harrison-2025-112"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Harrison-2025-112 id=mwAtE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAtI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAtM>[</span>105<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAtQ>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt355 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Elia-Shalev-2025_113-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Elia-Shalev-2025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Elia-Shalev-2025-113"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Elia-Shalev-2025-113 id=mwAtU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAtY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAtc>[</span>106<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAtg>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section><section data-mw-section-id=9 id=mwAtk aria-labelledby=Review_of_changes><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=Review_of_changes>Review of changes</h3></div>
<figure class=mw-default-size typeof=mw:File/Thumb id=mwAto><a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Wikipedia_editing_interface.png class=mw-file-description id=mwAts><img resource=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Wikipedia_editing_interface.png src=data:image/png;base64, decoding=async loading=lazy data-file-width=1920 data-file-height=1080 data-file-type=bitmap height=141 width=250 class=mw-file-element id=mwAtw></a><figcaption id=mwAt0>Wikipedia's editing interface</figcaption></figure><p id=mwAt4>Although changes are not systematically reviewed, Wikipedia's software provides tools allowing anyone to review changes made by others. Each article's History page links to each revision.<sup about=#mwt356 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-114 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-114"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Revisions with libelous content, criminal threats, or copyright infringements may be removed completely."}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-114 data-mw-group=lower-alpha id=mwAt8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAuA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAuE>[</span>h<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAuI>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt359 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Torsten_Kleinz_115-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Torsten_Kleinz"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Torsten_Kleinz-115 id=mwAuM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAuQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAuU>[</span>107<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAuY>]</span></span></a></sup> On most articles, anyone can view the latest changes and undo others' revisions by clicking a link on the article's History page. Registered users may maintain a "watchlist" of articles that interest them so they can be notified of changes.<sup about=#mwt360 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-116 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-116"}}'><a href=#cite_note-116 id=mwAuc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAug><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAuk>[</span>108<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAuo>]</span></span></a></sup> There are a variety of different 'patrols' on Wikipedia,<sup about=#mwt361 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-117 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-117"}}'><a href=#cite_note-117 id=mwAus><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAuw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAu0>[</span>109<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAu4>]</span></span></a></sup> including "New pages patrol" a patrol where newly created articles are checked for glaring issues.<sup about=#mwt362 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-118 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-118"}}'><a href=#cite_note-118 id=mwAu8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAvA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAvE>[</span>110<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAvI>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAvM>In 2003, economics PhD student Andrea Ciffolilli argued that the low <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transaction_cost title="Transaction cost" id=mwAvQ>transaction costs</a> of participating in a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiki title=Wiki id=mwAvU>wiki</a> created a catalyst for collaborative development, and that features such as allowing easy access to past versions of a page favored "creative construction" over "creative destruction".<sup about=#mwt365 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FMonday_collaborative_effort_1_119-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"FMonday collaborative effort 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FMonday_collaborative_effort_1-119"}}'><a href=#cite_note-FMonday_collaborative_effort_1-119 id=mwAvY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAvc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAvg>[</span>111<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAvk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section><section data-mw-section-id=10 id=mwAvo aria-labelledby=Vandalism><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=Vandalism>Vandalism</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt366 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwAvs data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Vandalism on Wikipedia"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237 about=#mwt367 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><div role=note class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about=#mwt366 id=mwAvw>Main article: <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vandalism_on_Wikipedia title="Vandalism on Wikipedia">Vandalism on Wikipedia</a></div>
<p id=mwAv0>Any change that deliberately compromises Wikipedia's integrity is considered vandalism. The most common and obvious types of vandalism include additions of obscenities and crude humor, but it can also include advertising and other types of spam.<sup about=#mwt370 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-upenn_link_spamming_1_120-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"upenn link spamming 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-upenn_link_spamming_1-120"}}'><a href=#cite_note-upenn_link_spamming_1-120 id=mwAv4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAv8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAwA>[</span>112<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAwE>]</span></span></a></sup> Sometimes editors commit vandalism by removing content or entirely blanking a given page. Less common types of vandalism, such as the deliberate addition of plausible but <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_information title="False information" class=mw-redirect id=mwAwI>false information</a>, can be more difficult to detect. Vandals can introduce irrelevant formatting, modify page semantics such as the page's title or categorization, manipulate the article's underlying code, or use images disruptively.<sup about=#mwt371 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-WP_vandalism_manipulation_1_121-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WP vandalism manipulation 1"}}'><a href=#cite_note-WP_vandalism_manipulation_1-121 id=mwAwM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAwQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAwU>[</span>113<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAwY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<figure class=mw-default-size typeof=mw:File/Thumb id=mwAwc><a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:John_Seigenthaler_Sr._speaking.jpg class=mw-file-description id=mwAwg><img resource=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:John_Seigenthaler_Sr._speaking.jpg src=data:image/jpeg;base64, decoding=async loading=lazy alt="White-haired elderly gentleman in suit and tie speaks at a podium." data-file-width=800 data-file-height=600 data-file-type=bitmap height=188 width=250 class=mw-file-element id=mwAwk></a><figcaption id=mwAwo>American journalist <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Seigenthaler title="John Seigenthaler" id=mwAws>John Seigenthaler</a> (1927–2014), subject of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seigenthaler_biography_incident title="Seigenthaler biography incident" class=mw-redirect id=mwAww>Seigenthaler biography incident</a></figcaption></figure>
<p id=mwAw0>Obvious vandalism is generally easy to remove from Wikipedia articles; the median time to detect and fix it is a few minutes.<sup about=#mwt372 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-MIT_IBM_study_122-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"MIT_IBM_study"}}'><a href=#cite_note-MIT_IBM_study-122 id=mwAw4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAw8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAxA>[</span>114<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAxE>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt373 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-CreatingDestroyingAndRestoringValue_123-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"CreatingDestroyingAndRestoringValue"}}'><a href=#cite_note-CreatingDestroyingAndRestoringValue-123 id=mwAxI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAxM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAxQ>[</span>115<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAxU>]</span></span></a></sup> However, some vandalism takes much longer to detect and repair.<sup about=#mwt374 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Seigenthaler_124-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Seigenthaler"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Seigenthaler-124 id=mwAxY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAxc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAxg>[</span>116<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAxk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAxo>In the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seigenthaler_biography_incident title="Seigenthaler biography incident" class=mw-redirect id=mwAxs>Seigenthaler biography incident</a>, an anonymous editor introduced false information into the biography of American political figure <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Seigenthaler title="John Seigenthaler" id=mwAxw>John Seigenthaler</a> in May 2005, falsely presenting him as a suspect in the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assassination_of_John_F._Kennedy title="Assassination of John F. Kennedy" id=mwAx0>assassination of John F. Kennedy</a>.<sup about=#mwt375 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Seigenthaler_124-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Seigenthaler"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Seigenthaler-124 id=mwAx4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAx8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAyA>[</span>116<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAyE>]</span></span></a></sup> It remained uncorrected for four months.<sup about=#mwt376 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Seigenthaler_124-2 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Seigenthaler"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Seigenthaler-124 id=mwAyI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAyM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAyQ>[</span>116<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAyU>]</span></span></a></sup> Seigenthaler, the founding editorial director of <i id=mwAyY><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USA_Today title="USA Today" id=mwAyc>USA Today</a></i> and founder of the Freedom Forum <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Amendment_Center title="First Amendment Center" id=mwAyg>First Amendment Center</a> at <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanderbilt_University title="Vanderbilt University" id=mwAyk>Vanderbilt University</a>, called Wikipedia co-founder Jimmy Wales and asked whether he had any way of knowing who contributed the misinformation. Wales said he did not, although the perpetrator was eventually traced.<sup about=#mwt379 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-book_The_World_is_Flat_1_125-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"book The World is Flat 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-book_The_World_is_Flat_1-125"}}'><a href=#cite_note-book_The_World_is_Flat_1-125 id=mwAyo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAys><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAyw>[</span>117<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAy0>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt382 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-126 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-126"}}'><a href=#cite_note-126 id=mwAy4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAy8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzA>[</span>118<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzE>]</span></span></a></sup> After the incident, Seigenthaler described Wikipedia as "a flawed and irresponsible research tool".<sup about=#mwt383 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Seigenthaler_124-3 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Seigenthaler"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Seigenthaler-124 id=mwAzI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAzM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzQ>[</span>116<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzU>]</span></span></a></sup> The incident led to policy changes at Wikipedia for tightening up the verifiability of biographical articles of living people.<sup about=#mwt386 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-127 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-127"}}'><a href=#cite_note-127 id=mwAzY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAzc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzg>[</span>119<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<div style=clear:left about=#mwt387 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwAzo data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Clear left","href":"./Template:Clear_left"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'></div>
</section><section data-mw-section-id=11 id=mwAzs aria-labelledby=Disputes_and_edit_warring><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=Disputes_and_edit_warring>Disputes and edit warring</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt388 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwAzw data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Disputes on Wikipedia"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237 about=#mwt389 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><div role=note class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about=#mwt388 id=mwAz0>Main article: <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disputes_on_Wikipedia title="Disputes on Wikipedia">Disputes on Wikipedia</a></div>
<p id=mwAz4>Wikipedia editors often have disagreements regarding content, which can be discussed on article Talk pages. Disputes may result in repeated competing changes to an article, known as "edit warring".<sup about=#mwt390 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Wikipedia:Dispute_resolution_–_Wikipedia_128-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Wikipedia:Dispute resolution – Wikipedia"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Wikipedia:Dispute_resolution_–_Wikipedia-128"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Wikipedia:Dispute_resolution_%E2%80%93_Wikipedia-128 id=mwAz8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA0A><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0E>[</span>120<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0I>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt393 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-NBC_WP_editorial_warzone_12_129-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NBC WP editorial warzone 12"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NBC_WP_editorial_warzone_12-129"}}'><a href=#cite_note-NBC_WP_editorial_warzone_12-129 id=mwA0M><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA0Q><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0U>[</span>121<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0Y>]</span></span></a></sup> It is widely seen as a resource-consuming scenario where no useful knowledge is added,<sup about=#mwt396 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-130 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-130"}}'><a href=#cite_note-130 id=mwA0c><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA0g><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0k>[</span>122<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0o>]</span></span></a></sup> and criticized as creating a competitive<sup about=#mwt399 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-131 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-131"}}'><a href=#cite_note-131 id=mwA0s><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA0w><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA00>[</span>123<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA04>]</span></span></a></sup> and conflict-based editing culture associated with traditional masculine <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gender_role title="Gender role" id=mwA08>gender roles</a>.<sup about=#mwt402 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-132 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-132"}}'><a href=#cite_note-132 id=mwA1A><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA1E><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA1I>[</span>124<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA1M>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt405 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-133 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-133"}}'><a href=#cite_note-133 id=mwA1Q><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA1U><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA1Y>[</span>125<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA1c>]</span></span></a></sup> Research has focused on, for example, impoliteness of disputes,<sup about=#mwt408 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-134 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-134"}}'><a href=#cite_note-134 id=mwA1g><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA1k><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA1o>[</span>126<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA1s>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt411 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-135 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-135"}}'><a href=#cite_note-135 id=mwA1w><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA10><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA14>[</span>127<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA18>]</span></span></a></sup> the influence of rival editing camps,<sup about=#mwt414 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-136 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-136"}}'><a href=#cite_note-136 id=mwA2A><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA2E><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA2I>[</span>128<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA2M>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt417 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-137 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-137"}}'><a href=#cite_note-137 id=mwA2Q><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA2U><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA2Y>[</span>129<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA2c>]</span></span></a></sup> the conversational structure,<sup about=#mwt420 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-138 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-138"}}'><a href=#cite_note-138 id=mwA2g><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA2k><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA2o>[</span>130<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA2s>]</span></span></a></sup> and the shift in conflicts to a focus on sources.<sup about=#mwt423 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-139 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-139"}}'><a href=#cite_note-139 id=mwA2w><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA20><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA24>[</span>131<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA28>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt426 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Ruprechter_140-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Ruprechter"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Ruprechter-140"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Ruprechter-140 id=mwA3A><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA3E><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA3I>[</span>132<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA3M>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA3Q><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taha_Yasseri title="Taha Yasseri" id=mwA3U>Taha Yasseri</a> of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Oxford title="University of Oxford" id=mwA3Y>University of Oxford</a> examined editing conflicts and their resolution in a 2013 study.<sup about=#mwt429 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-141 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-141"}}'><a href=#cite_note-141 id=mwA3c><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA3g><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA3k>[</span>133<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA3o>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt432 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Yasseri-2014_142-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Yasseri-2014"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Yasseri-2014-142"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Yasseri-2014-142 id=mwA3s><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA3w><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA30>[</span>134<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA34>]</span></span></a></sup> Yasseri contended that simple reverts or "undo" operations were not the most significant measure of <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Counterproductive_work_behavior title="Counterproductive work behavior" id=mwA38>counterproductive work behavior</a> at Wikipedia. He relied instead on "mutually reverting edit pairs", where one editor reverts the edit of another editor who then, in sequence, returns to revert the first editor. The results were tabulated for several language versions of Wikipedia. The English Wikipedia's three largest conflict rates belonged to the articles <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_W._Bush title="George W. Bush" id=mwA4A>George W. Bush</a>, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anarchism title=Anarchism id=mwA4E>anarchism</a>, and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muhammad title=Muhammad id=mwA4I>Muhammad</a>.<sup about=#mwt433 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Yasseri-2014_142-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Yasseri-2014"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Yasseri-2014-142 id=mwA4M><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA4Q><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA4U>[</span>134<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA4Y>]</span></span></a></sup> By comparison, for the German Wikipedia, the three largest conflict rates at the time of the study were for the articles covering <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croatia title=Croatia id=mwA4c>Croatia</a>, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientology title=Scientology id=mwA4g>Scientology</a>, and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/9/11_conspiracy_theories title="9/11 conspiracy theories" id=mwA4k>9/11 conspiracy theories</a>.<sup about=#mwt434 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Yasseri-2014_142-2 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Yasseri-2014"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Yasseri-2014-142 id=mwA4o><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA4s><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA4w>[</span>134<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA40>]</span></span></a></sup> In 2020, researchers identified other measures of editor behaviors, beyond mutual reverts, to identify editing conflicts across Wikipedia.<sup about=#mwt435 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Ruprechter_140-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Ruprechter"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Ruprechter-140 id=mwA44><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA48><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA5A>[</span>132<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA5E>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA5I>Editors also debate the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deletion_of_articles_on_Wikipedia title="Deletion of articles on Wikipedia" id=mwA5M>deletion of articles on Wikipedia</a>, with roughly 500,000 such debates since Wikipedia's inception as of 2019. Once an article is nominated for deletion, the dispute is typically determined by initial votes (to keep or delete) and by reference to topic-specific notability policies.<sup about=#mwt438 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-143 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-143"}}'><a href=#cite_note-143 id=mwA5Q><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA5U><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA5Y>[</span>135<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA5c>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section></section><section data-mw-section-id=12 id=mwA5g aria-labelledby=Policies_and_content><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=Policies_and_content><span class=anchor id=Rules_and_laws_governing_content_and_editor_behavior></span><span class=anchor id=Rules_and_laws_governing_content></span><span class=anchor id=Censorship></span> Policies and content</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt439 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwA5k data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"self-reference","href":"./Template:Self-reference"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"\"Five pillars of Wikipedia\" redirects here. For the Wikipedia policy, see [[Wikipedia:Five pillars]]."}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237 about=#mwt440 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><div role=note class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable plainlinks selfreference noprint" about=#mwt439 id=mwA5o>"Five pillars of Wikipedia" redirects here. For the Wikipedia policy, see <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Five_pillars title="Wikipedia:Five pillars">Wikipedia:Five pillars</a>.</div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt441 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwA5s data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"External media","href":"./Template:External_media"},"params":{"width":{"wt":"220px"},"float":{"wt":"right"},"headerimage":{"wt":"[[File:Jimbo at Fosdem cropped.jpg|210px|alt=photograph]]"},"video1":{"wt":"[https://www.ted.com/talks/jimmy_wales_the_birth_of_wikipedia?language=en Jimmy Wales], The Birth of Wikipedia, 2006, [[TED talk]]s, 20 minutes"},"video2":{"wt":"[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r2gsj0EEE3I Katherine Maher], What Wikipedia Teaches Us About Balancing Truth and Beliefs, 2022, [[TED talk]]s, 15 minutes"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1358620234 about=#mwt442 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Infobox/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><table class=infobox style="width:220px;clear:right;float:right;margin:0 0 4px 1.5em" about=#mwt441 id=mwA5w><tbody><tr><th colspan=2 class=infobox-above style=font-size:115%>External videos<tr><td colspan=2 class=infobox-image><span typeof=mw:File><a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Jimbo_at_Fosdem_cropped.jpg class=mw-file-description><img resource=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Jimbo_at_Fosdem_cropped.jpg src="data:image/jpeg;base64," decoding=async loading=lazy alt=photograph data-file-width=1536 data-file-height=1786 data-file-type=bitmap height=244 width=210 class=mw-file-element></a></span><tr><td colspan=2 class=infobox-full-data style=text-align:left><span class=noviewer typeof=mw:File><a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Nuvola_apps_kaboodle.svg class=mw-file-description><img resource=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Nuvola_apps_kaboodle.svg src='data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20"><rect fill-opacity="0"/></svg>' decoding=async loading=lazy alt="video icon" data-file-width=128 data-file-height=128 data-file-type=drawing height=16 width=16 class=mw-file-element style="background-blend-mode:normal!important;background-clip:content-box!important;background-position:50% 50%!important;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0)!important;background-image:var(--sf-img-27)!important;background-size:100% 100%!important;background-origin:content-box!important;background-repeat:no-repeat!important;background-attachment:scroll!important"></a></span> <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.ted.com/talks/jimmy_wales_the_birth_of_wikipedia?language=en" class="external text">Jimmy Wales</a>, The Birth of Wikipedia, 2006, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TED_talk title="TED talk" class=mw-redirect>TED talks</a>, 20 minutes<tr><td colspan=2 class=infobox-full-data style=text-align:left><span class=noviewer typeof=mw:File><a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Nuvola_apps_kaboodle.svg class=mw-file-description><img resource=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Nuvola_apps_kaboodle.svg src='data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20"><rect fill-opacity="0"/></svg>' decoding=async loading=lazy alt="video icon" data-file-width=128 data-file-height=128 data-file-type=drawing height=16 width=16 class=mw-file-element style="background-blend-mode:normal!important;background-clip:content-box!important;background-position:50% 50%!important;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0)!important;background-image:var(--sf-img-27)!important;background-size:100% 100%!important;background-origin:content-box!important;background-repeat:no-repeat!important;background-attachment:scroll!important"></a></span> <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r2gsj0EEE3I" class="external text">Katherine Maher</a>, What Wikipedia Teaches Us About Balancing Truth and Beliefs, 2022, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TED_talk title="TED talk" class=mw-redirect>TED talks</a>, 15 minutes</table>
<p id=mwA50>Wikipedia is composed of 11 different <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Namespace title=Namespace id=mwA54>namespaces</a>,<sup class="noprint Inline-Template" style=white-space:nowrap about=#mwt443 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwA58 data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Update inline","href":"./Template:Update_inline"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"August 2026"},"reason":{"wt":"WP:Namespace claims that \"Wikipedia has 30 current namespaces\". This is far more than 11."}},"i":0}}]}'><span typeof=mw:Entity>[</span><i><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Manual_of_Style/Dates_and_numbers#Chronological_items title="Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Dates and numbers"><span title='WP:Namespace claims that "Wikipedia has 30 current namespaces". This is far more than 11. (August 2026)'>needs update</span></a></i><span typeof=mw:Entity>]</span></sup><span typeof=mw:Nowiki about=#mwt443></span><link rel=mw:PageProp/Category href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Wikipedia_articles_in_need_of_updating_from_August_2026 about=#mwt443><link rel=mw:PageProp/Category href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:All_Wikipedia_articles_in_need_of_updating about=#mwt443 id=mwA6A> with its articles being present in <i id=mwA6E>mainspace</i>. Other namespaces have a prefix before their page title and fulfill various purposes. For example, the project namespace uses the <i id=mwA6I>Wikipedia</i> prefix and is used for self-governance-related discussions. Most readers are not aware of these other namespaces.<sup about=#mwt444 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter20223_144-0 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter20223"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter20223-144"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"page":{"wt":"3"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter20223-144 id=mwA6M><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA6Q><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA6U>[</span>136<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA6Y>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA6c>The fundamental principles of the Wikipedia community are embodied in the "Five Pillars", while the detailed editorial principles are expressed in numerous policies and guidelines intended to appropriately shape content.<sup about=#mwt447 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-145 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-145"}}'><a href=#cite_note-145 id=mwA6g><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA6k><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA6o>[</span>137<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA6s>]</span></span></a></sup> The five pillars are:</p>
<ul id=mwA6w><li id=mwA60>Wikipedia is an encyclopedia</li>
<li id=mwA64>Wikipedia is written from a neutral point of view</li>
<li id=mwA68>Wikipedia is free content that anyone can use, edit, and distribute</li>
<li id=mwA7A>Wikipedia's editors should treat each other with respect and civility</li>
<li id=mwA7E>Wikipedia has no firm rules</ul>
<p id=mwA7I>The rules developed by the community are stored in the Wikipedia namespace, and Wikipedia editors write and revise the website's policies and guidelines in accordance with community consensus.<sup about=#mwt450 class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-pcworld_who's_behind_WP_146-0" rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw="{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;pcworld who's behind WP&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-pcworld_who's_behind_WP-146&quot;}}"><a href="#cite_note-pcworld_who's_behind_WP-146" id=mwA7M><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA7Q><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA7U>[</span>138<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA7Y>]</span></span></a></sup> Originally, rules on the non-English editions of Wikipedia were based on a translation of the rules for the English Wikipedia. They have since diverged to some extent.<sup about=#mwt451 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-WP_some_sites_stable_versions_1_106-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WP some sites stable versions 1"}}'><a href=#cite_note-WP_some_sites_stable_versions_1-106 id=mwA7c><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA7g><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA7k>[</span>99<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA7o>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<section data-mw-section-id=13 id=mwA7s aria-labelledby=Content_policies_and_guidelines><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=Content_policies_and_guidelines>Content policies and guidelines</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt452 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwA7w data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Self-reference","href":"./Template:Self-reference"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"\"No original research\" redirects here. For the Wikipedia policy, see [[Wikipedia:No original research]]."}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237 about=#mwt453 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><div role=note class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable plainlinks selfreference noprint" about=#mwt452 id=mwA70>"No original research" redirects here. For the Wikipedia policy, see <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:No_original_research title="Wikipedia:No original research">Wikipedia:No original research</a>.</div>
<p id=mwA74>According to the rules on the English Wikipedia community, each entry in Wikipedia must be about a topic that is <a rel=mw:WikiLink/Interwiki href=https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/encyclopedic title=wikt:encyclopedic class=extiw id=mwA78>encyclopedic</a> and is not a dictionary entry or dictionary-style.<sup about=#mwt469 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-:5_147-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":5"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:5-147"}}'><a href=#cite_note-:5-147 id=mwA8A><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA8E><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8I>[</span>139<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8M>]</span></span></a></sup> A topic should also meet <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Notability_in_the_English_Wikipedia title="Notability in the English Wikipedia" class=mw-redirect id=mwA8Q>Wikipedia's standards of "notability"</a>, which generally means that the topic has been covered extensively in reliable sources that are independent of the article's subject.<sup about=#mwt454 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202253–54_148-0 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202253–54"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202253–54-148"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"pages":{"wt":"53–54"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202253%E2%80%9354-148 id=mwA8U><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA8Y><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8c>[</span>140<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8g>]</span></span></a></sup> Wikipedia intends to convey only knowledge that is already established and recognized and therefore must not present original research.<sup about=#mwt457 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202232_149-0 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202232"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202232-149"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"page":{"wt":"32"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202232-149 id=mwA8k><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA8o><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8s>[</span>141<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8w>]</span></span></a></sup> Some subjects, such as politicians and academics, have specialized notability requirements.<sup about=#mwt460 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202253–54_148-1 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202253–54"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202253–54-148"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"pages":{"wt":"53–54"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202253%E2%80%9354-148 id=mwA80><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA84><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA88>[</span>140<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9A>]</span></span></a></sup> Finally, Wikipedia must reflect a neutral point of view. This is accomplished through summarizing reliable sources, using impartial language, and ensuring that multiple points of view are presented based on their prominence. Information must also be verifiable.<sup about=#mwt463 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter20225–7_150-0 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter20225–7"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter20225–7-150"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"pages":{"wt":"5–7"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter20225%E2%80%937-150 id=mwA9E><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA9I><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9M>[</span>142<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9Q>]</span></span></a></sup> Information without citations may be <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Citation_needed_(Wikipedia) title="Citation needed (Wikipedia)" class=mw-redirect id=mwA9U>tagged</a> or removed entirely.<sup about=#mwt466 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202233_151-0 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202233"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202233-151"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"page":{"wt":"33"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202233-151 id=mwA9Y><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA9c><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9g>[</span>143<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9k>]</span></span></a></sup> This can at times lead to the removal of information which, though valid, is not properly sourced.<sup about=#mwt472 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-IHT_WP_valid_info_wrong_removable_1_152-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"IHT WP valid info wrong removable 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-IHT_WP_valid_info_wrong_removable_1-152"}}'><a href=#cite_note-IHT_WP_valid_info_wrong_removable_1-152 id=mwA9o><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA9s><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9w>[</span>144<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA90>]</span></span></a></sup> As Wikipedia policies changed over time, and became more complex, their number has grown. In 2008, there were 44 policy pages and 248 guideline pages; by 2013, scholars counted 383 policy pages and 449 guideline pages.<sup about=#mwt473 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Ren-2024_104-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Ren-2024"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Ren-2024-104 id=mwA94><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA98><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-A>[</span>97<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-E>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section></section><section data-mw-section-id=14 id=mwA-I aria-labelledby=Governance><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=Governance>Governance</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt474 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwA-M data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wikipedia:Administration"},"selfref":{"wt":"yes"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237 about=#mwt475 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><div role=note class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable selfref" about=#mwt474 id=mwA-Q>Further information: <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Administration title=Wikipedia:Administration>Wikipedia:Administration</a></div>
<p id=mwA-U>Wikipedia's initial anarchy integrated democratic and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierarchy title=Hierarchy id=mwA-Y>hierarchical</a> elements over time.<sup about=#mwt481 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-153 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-153"}}'><a href=#cite_note-153 id=mwA-c><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA-g><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-k>[</span>145<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-o>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt484 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-154 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-154"}}'><a href=#cite_note-154 id=mwA-s><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA-w><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-0>[</span>146<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-4>]</span></span></a></sup> An article is not considered to be owned by its creator or any other editor, nor by the subject of the article.<sup about=#mwt485 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-155 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-155"}}'><a href=#cite_note-155 id=mwA-8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA_A><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_E>[</span>147<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_I>]</span></span></a></sup> Editors in good standing in the community can request extra user rights, granting them the technical ability to perform certain special actions. Some user rights are granted automatically, such as the <i id=mwA_M>autoconfirmed</i> and <i id=mwA_Q>extended confirmed</i> groups, when thresholds for account age and edits are met.<sup about=#mwt476 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202212–13_101-1 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202212–13"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202212–13-101"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"pages":{"wt":"12–13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202212%E2%80%9313-101 id=mwA_U><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA_Y><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_c>[</span>94<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_g>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<section data-mw-section-id=15 id=mwA_k aria-labelledby=Administrators><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=Administrators>Administrators</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt486 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwA_o data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wikipedia administrators"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237 about=#mwt487 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><div role=note class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about=#mwt486 id=mwA_s>Main article: <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia_administrators title="Wikipedia administrators">Wikipedia administrators</a></div>
<p id=mwA_w>Experienced editors can choose to run for "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Administrators_(Wikipedia) title="Administrators (Wikipedia)" class=mw-redirect id=mwA_0>adminship</a>",<sup about=#mwt488 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-David_Mehegan_156-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"David_Mehegan"}}'><a href=#cite_note-David_Mehegan-156 id=mwA_4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA_8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBAA>[</span>148<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBAE>]</span></span></a></sup> which includes the ability to delete pages or prevent them from being changed in cases of severe vandalism or editorial disputes.<sup about=#mwt489 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-:6_157-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":6"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:6-157"}}'><a href=#cite_note-:6-157 id=mwBAI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBAM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBAQ>[</span>149<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBAU>]</span></span></a></sup> Administrators are not supposed to enjoy any special privilege in decision-making; instead, their powers are mostly limited to making edits that have project-wide effects and thus are disallowed to ordinary editors, and to implement restrictions intended to prevent disruptive editors from making unproductive edits.<sup about=#mwt490 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-:6_157-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":6"}}'><a href=#cite_note-:6-157 id=mwBAY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBAc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBAg>[</span>149<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBAk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBAo>By 2012, fewer editors were becoming administrators compared to Wikipedia's earlier years, in part because the process of vetting potential administrators had become more rigorous.<sup about=#mwt491 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Meyer-2012_54-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Meyer-2012"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Meyer-2012-54 id=mwBAs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBAw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBA0>[</span>48<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBA4>]</span></span></a></sup> In 2022, there was a particularly contentious request for adminship over the candidate's anti-Trump views; ultimately, they were granted adminship.<sup about=#mwt494 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-158 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-158"}}'><a href=#cite_note-158 id=mwBA8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBBA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBBE>[</span>150<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBBI>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section><section data-mw-section-id=16 id=mwBBM aria-labelledby=Dispute_resolution><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=Dispute_resolution>Dispute resolution</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt495 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwBBQ data-mw="{&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;template&quot;:{&quot;target&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;Redirect&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:&quot;./Template:Redirect&quot;},&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;!vote&quot;},&quot;2&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;the entry in Wikipedia's internal glossary&quot;},&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;Wikipedia:Glossary#!vote&quot;},&quot;4&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;the internal essay&quot;},&quot;5&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;Wikipedia:Polling is not a substitute for discussion&quot;},&quot;selfref&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;yes&quot;}},&quot;i&quot;:0}}]}"><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237 about=#mwt496 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><div role=note class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable selfref" about=#mwt495 id=mwBBU>"!vote" redirects here. For the entry in Wikipedia's internal glossary, see <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Glossary#!vote title=Wikipedia:Glossary>Wikipedia:Glossary §<span typeof=mw:Entity>&nbsp;</span>!vote</a>. For the internal essay, see <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Polling_is_not_a_substitute_for_discussion title="Wikipedia:Polling is not a substitute for discussion">Wikipedia:Polling is not a substitute for discussion</a>.</div>
<p id=mwBBY>Over time, Wikipedia has developed a semi-formal dispute resolution process. To determine community consensus, editors can raise issues at appropriate community forums, seek outside input through third opinion requests, or initiate a more general community discussion known as a "request for comment",<sup about=#mwt497 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Wikipedia:Dispute_resolution_–_Wikipedia_128-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Wikipedia:Dispute resolution – Wikipedia"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Wikipedia:Dispute_resolution_%E2%80%93_Wikipedia-128 id=mwBBc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBBg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBBk>[</span>120<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBBo>]</span></span></a></sup> in which bots add the discussion to a centralized list of discussions, invite editors to participate, and remove the discussion from the list after 30 days.<sup about=#mwt498 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-159 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-159"}}'><a href=#cite_note-159 id=mwBBs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBBw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBB0>[</span>151<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBB4>]</span></span></a></sup> However, editors have the discretion to close (and delist) the discussion early or late. If the result of a discussion is not obvious, a closer—an uninvolved editor usually in good standing—may render a verdict from the strength of the arguments presented and then the number of arguers on each side.<sup about=#mwt501 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Bandler-2024_160-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Bandler-2024"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Bandler-2024-160"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Bandler-2024-160 id=mwBB8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBCA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBCE>[</span>152<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBCI>]</span></span></a></sup> Wikipedians emphasize that the process is not a vote by referring to statements of opinion in such discussions as "!vote"s, in which the exclamation mark is the symbol for <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negation#Usage_in_colloquial_language title=Negation id=mwBCM>logical negation</a> and pronounced "not".<sup about=#mwt504 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-161 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-161"}}'><a href=#cite_note-161 id=mwBCQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBCU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBCY>[</span>153<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBCc>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBCg>Wikipedia encourages local resolutions of conflicts, which Jemielniak argues is quite unique in organization studies, though there has been some recent interest in <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consensus_decision-making title="Consensus decision-making" id=mwBCk>consensus building</a> in the field.<sup about=#mwt507 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Jemielniak_162-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Jemielniak"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Jemielniak-162 id=mwBCo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBCs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBCw>[</span>154<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBC0>]</span></span></a></sup> Reagle and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sue_Gardner title="Sue Gardner" id=mwBC4>Sue Gardner</a> argue that the approaches to consensus building are similar to those used by <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quakers title=Quakers id=mwBC8>Quakers</a>.<sup about=#mwt508 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Jemielniak_162-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Jemielniak"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Jemielniak-162 id=mwBDA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBDE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBDI>[</span>154<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBDM>]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about=#mwt505 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwBDQ data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"page":{"wt":"62"}},"i":0}}]}'><span title="Page: 62"><span typeof=mw:Entity>:</span><span typeof=mw:Entity> </span>62<span typeof=mw:Entity> </span></span></sup> A difference from Quaker meetings is the absence of a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facilitator title=Facilitator id=mwBDU>facilitator</a> in the presence of disagreement, a role played by the clerk in Quaker meetings.<sup about=#mwt509 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Jemielniak_162-2 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Jemielniak"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Jemielniak-162 id=mwBDY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBDc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBDg>[</span>154<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBDk>]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about=#mwt506 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwBDo data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"page":{"wt":"83"}},"i":0}}]}'><span title="Page: 83"><span typeof=mw:Entity>:</span><span typeof=mw:Entity> </span>83<span typeof=mw:Entity> </span></span></sup></p>
<section data-mw-section-id=17 id=mwBDs aria-labelledby=Arbitration_Committee><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=Arbitration_Committee>Arbitration Committee</h4></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt510 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwBDw data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Arbitration Committee (Wikipedia)"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237 about=#mwt511 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><div role=note class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about=#mwt510 id=mwBD0>Main article: <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbitration_Committee_(Wikipedia) title="Arbitration Committee (Wikipedia)" class=mw-redirect>Arbitration Committee (Wikipedia)</a></div>
<p id=mwBD4>The Arbitration Committee presides over the ultimate dispute resolution process. Although disputes usually arise from a disagreement between two opposing views on how an article should read, the Arbitration Committee explicitly refuses to directly rule on the specific view that should be adopted.<sup about=#mwt514 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Hoffman-2009_163-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Hoffman-2009"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Hoffman-2009-163"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Hoffman-2009-163 id=mwBD8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBEA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEE>[</span>155<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEI>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBEM>Statistical analyses suggest that the English Wikipedia committee ignores the content of disputes and rather focuses on the way disputes are conducted,<sup about=#mwt518 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-emory_p_181_164-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"emory_p_181"}}'><a href=#cite_note-emory_p_181-164 id=mwBEQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBEU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEY>[</span>156<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEc>]</span></span></a></sup> functioning not so much to resolve disputes and make peace between conflicting editors, but to weed out problematic editors while allowing potentially productive editors back in to participate.<sup about=#mwt519 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Hoffman-2009_163-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Hoffman-2009"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Hoffman-2009-163 id=mwBEg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBEk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEo>[</span>155<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEs>]</span></span></a></sup> Therefore, the committee does not dictate the content of articles, although it sometimes condemns content changes when it deems the new content violates Wikipedia policies (for example, if the new content is considered biased).<sup about=#mwt515 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-165 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-165"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"The committee may directly rule that a content change is inappropriate, but may not directly rule that certain content is inappropriate."}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-165 data-mw-group=lower-alpha id=mwBEw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBE0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBE4>[</span>i<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBE8>]</span></span></a></sup> Commonly used solutions include cautions and probations (used in 63% of cases) and banning editors from articles (43%), subject matters (23%), or Wikipedia (16%).<sup about=#mwt520 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Hoffman-2009_163-2 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Hoffman-2009"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Hoffman-2009-163 id=mwBFA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBFE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFI>[</span>155<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFM>]</span></span></a></sup> Complete bans from Wikipedia are generally limited to instances of <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impersonator title=Impersonator id=mwBFQ>impersonation</a> and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-social_behavior title="Anti-social behavior" class=mw-redirect id=mwBFU>anti-social behavior</a>.<sup about=#mwt521 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-166 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-166"}}'><a href=#cite_note-166 id=mwBFY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBFc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFg>[</span>157<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFk>]</span></span></a></sup> When conduct is not impersonation or anti-social, but rather edit warring and other violations of editing policies, solutions tend to be limited to warnings.<sup about=#mwt522 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Hoffman-2009_163-3 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Hoffman-2009"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Hoffman-2009-163 id=mwBFo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBFs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFw>[</span>155<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBF0>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section></section></section><section data-mw-section-id=18 id=mwBF4 aria-labelledby=Community><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=Community>Community</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt sf-hidden" about=#mwt523 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwBF8 data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wikipedia community"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237 about=#mwt524 typeof=mw:Extension/templatestyles data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'></span><div role=note class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about=#mwt523 id=mwBGA>Main article: <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia_community title="Wikipedia community">Wikipedia community</a></div>
<figure class=mw-default-size typeof=mw:File/Thumb id=mwBGE data-mw='{"attribs":[["starttime",{"txt":"00:04"}],["thumbtime",{"txt":"00:16"}]]}'><span id=mwBGI><span class="mw-tmh-player video"><video poster=data:image/webp;base64, preload=none data-mw-tmh height=191 width=250 resource=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Wikimania_-_the_Wikimentary.webm data-durationhint=490 class=mw-file-element id=mwBGM_placeholder playsinline disabled tabindex=-1 style=aspect-ratio:250/191></video><a class=mw-tmh-play href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Wikimania_-_the_Wikimentary.webm title="Play video" role=button><span class="mw-tmh-play-icon notheme"></span></a><span class="mw-tmh-duration mw-tmh-label"><span class=sr-only>Duration: 8 minutes and 10 seconds.</span><span aria-hidden=true>8:10</span></span><span class="mw-tmh-cc mw-tmh-label"><span class=sr-only>Subtitles available.</span><span aria-hidden=true>CC</span></span></span></span><a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Wikimania_-_the_Wikimentary.webm class=mw-file-magnify></a><figcaption id=mwBGo>Video of <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikimania#2005 title=Wikimania id=mwBGs>Wikimania 2005</a><span typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Entity" about=#mwt525 id=mwBGw data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"snd","href":"./Template:Snd"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'>&nbsp;</span><span typeof=mw:Entity about=#mwt525>–</span><span typeof=mw:Entity about=#mwt525> </span>an annual conference for users of Wikipedia and other projects operated by the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikimedia_Foundation title="Wikimedia Foundation" id=mwBG0>Wikimedia Foundation</a>, was held in <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankfurt_am_Main title="Frankfurt am Main" class=mw-redirect id=mwBG4>Frankfurt am Main</a>, Germany, August 4–8.</figcaption></figure>
<p id=mwBG8>Each article and each user of Wikipedia has an associated and dedicated "talk" page. These form the primary communication channel for editors to discuss, coordinate, and debate.<sup about=#mwt528 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-167 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-167"}}'><a href=#cite_note-167 id=mwBHA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBHE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHI>[</span>158<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHM>]</span></span></a></sup> Wikipedia's community has been described as <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cult title=Cult id=mwBHQ>cultlike</a>,<sup about=#mwt531 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-168 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-168"}}'><a href=#cite_note-168 id=mwBHU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBHY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHc>[</span>159<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHg>]</span></span></a></sup> although not always with entirely negative connotations.<sup about=#mwt534 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-169 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-169"}}'><a href=#cite_note-169 id=mwBHk><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBHo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHs>[</span>160<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHw>]</span></span></a></sup> Its preference for cohesiveness, even if it requires compromise that includes disregard of <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Credential title=Credential id=mwBH0>credentials</a>, has been referred to as "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-elitism title=Anti-elitism id=mwBH4>anti-elitism</a>".<sup about=#mwt537 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-170 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-170"}}'><a href=#cite_note-170 id=mwBH8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBIA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBIE>[</span>161<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBII>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof=mw:File/Thumb id=mwBIM><span id=mwBIQ><span class="mw-tmh-player video"><video poster="data:image/webp;base64," preload=none data-mw-tmh height=168 width=250 resource=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Editing_Hoxne_Hoard_at_the_British_Museum.ogv data-durationhint=60 class=mw-file-element id=mwBIU_placeholder playsinline disabled tabindex=-1 style=aspect-ratio:250/168></video><a class=mw-tmh-play href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Editing_Hoxne_Hoard_at_the_British_Museum.ogv title="Play video" role=button><span class="mw-tmh-play-icon notheme"></span></a><span class="mw-tmh-duration mw-tmh-label"><span class=sr-only>Duration: 1 minute and 0 seconds.</span><span aria-hidden=true>1:00</span></span></span></span><a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Editing_Hoxne_Hoard_at_the_British_Museum.ogv class=mw-file-magnify></a><figcaption id=mwBIs>Wikipedians and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Museum title="British Museum" id=mwBIw>British Museum</a> curators collaborate on the article <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hoxne_Hoard title="Hoxne Hoard" id=mwBI0>Hoxne Hoard</a> in June 2010.</figcaption></figure>
<p id=mwBI4>Wikipedia does not require that its editors and contributors provide identification.<sup about=#mwt541 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-user_identification_171-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"user identification"}}'><a href=#cite_note-user_identification-171 id=mwBI8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBJA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJE>[</span>162<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJI>]</span></span></a></sup> As Wikipedia grew, "Who writes Wikipedia?" became one of the questions frequently asked there.<sup about=#mwt544 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-172 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-172"}}'><a href=#cite_note-172 id=mwBJM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBJQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJU>[</span>163<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJY>]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jimmy_Wales title="Jimmy Wales" id=mwBJc>Jimmy Wales</a> once argued that only "a community<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwBJg>&nbsp;</span>... a dedicated group of a few hundred volunteers" makes the bulk of contributions to Wikipedia and that the project is therefore "much like any traditional organization".<sup about=#mwt547 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-blodget_173-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"blodget"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-blodget-173"}}'><a href=#cite_note-blodget-173 id=mwBJk><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBJo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJs>[</span>164<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJw>]</span></span></a></sup> Since Wikipedia relies on volunteer labour, editors frequently focus on topics that interest them.<sup about=#mwt538 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202261–62_174-0 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202261–62"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202261–62-174"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"pages":{"wt":"61–62"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202261%E2%80%9362-174 id=mwBJ0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBJ4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJ8>[</span>165<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBKA>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBKE><span class=anchor id=Decline_in_participation_since_2007 about=#mwt548 typeof=mw:Transclusion data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"anchor","href":"./Template:Anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Decline in participation since 2007"}},"i":0}}]}'></span>
The English Wikipedia has <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Statistics title=Special:Statistics about=#mwt549 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwBKI data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"self-reference link","href":"./Template:Self-reference_link"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Special:Statistics"},"2":{"wt":"{{NUMBEROFARTICLES}}"}},"i":0}}]}'>7,253,617</a> articles, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:ListUsers title=Special:ListUsers about=#mwt550 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwBKM data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"self-reference link","href":"./Template:Self-reference_link"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Special:ListUsers"},"2":{"wt":"{{NUMBEROFUSERS}}"}},"i":0}}]}'>54,994,838</a> registered editors, and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:ActiveUsers title=Special:ActiveUsers about=#mwt551 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwBKQ data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"self-reference link","href":"./Template:Self-reference_link"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Special:ActiveUsers"},"2":{"wt":"{{NUMBEROFACTIVEUSERS}}"}},"i":0}}]}'>271,829</a> active editors. An editor is considered active if they have made one or more edits in the past 30<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwBKU>&nbsp;</span>days.<sup about=#mwt555 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-175 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-175"}}'><a href=#cite_note-175 id=mwBKY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBKc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBKg>[</span>166<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBKk>]</span></span></a></sup> Editors who fail to comply with Wikipedia cultural rituals, such as signing talk page comments, may implicitly signal that they are Wikipedia outsiders, increasing the odds that Wikipedia insiders may target or discount their contributions. Becoming a Wikipedia insider involves non-trivial costs: the contributor is expected to learn Wikipedia-specific technological codes, submit to a sometimes convoluted dispute resolution process, and learn a "baffling culture rich with in-jokes and insider references".<sup about=#mwt556 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-labor_squeeze_on_WP_1_176-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"labor squeeze on WP 1"}}'><a href=#cite_note-labor_squeeze_on_WP_1-176 id=mwBKo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBKs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBKw>[</span>167<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBK0>]</span></span></a></sup> Editors who do not log in are in some sense "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second-class_citizen title="Second-class citizen" id=mwBK4>second-class citizens</a>" on Wikipedia,<sup about=#mwt559 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-labor_squeeze_on_WP_1_176-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"labor squeeze on WP 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-labor_squeeze_on_WP_1-176"}}'><a href=#cite_note-labor_squeeze_on_WP_1-176 id=mwBK8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBLA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLE>[</span>167<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLI>]</span></span></a></sup> as "participants are accredited by members of the wiki community, who have a vested interest in preserving the quality of the work product, on the basis of their ongoing participation",<sup about=#mwt562 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-legal_edu_and_WP_1_177-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"legal edu and WP 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-legal_edu_and_WP_1-177"}}'><a href=#cite_note-legal_edu_and_WP_1-177 id=mwBLM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBLQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLU>[</span>168<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLY>]</span></span></a></sup> but the contribution histories of anonymous unregistered editors recognized only by their <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IP_address title="IP address" id=mwBLc>IP addresses</a> cannot be attributed to a particular editor with certainty.<sup about=#mwt563 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-legal_edu_and_WP_1_177-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"legal edu and WP 1"}}'><a href=#cite_note-legal_edu_and_WP_1-177 id=mwBLg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBLk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLo>[</span>168<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLs>]</span></span></a></sup> New editors often struggle to understand Wikipedia's complexity. Experienced editors are encouraged not to "bite" the newcomers in order to create a more welcoming atmosphere.<sup about=#mwt552 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202210–11_178-0 rel=dc:references typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202210–11"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202210–11-178"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McDowell"},"2":{"wt":"Vetter"},"3":{"wt":"2022"},"pages":{"wt":"10–11"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href=#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcDowellVetter202210%E2%80%9311-178 id=mwBLw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBL0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBL4>[</span>169<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBL8>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<section data-mw-section-id=19 id=mwBMA aria-labelledby=Research><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=Research>Research</h3></div>
<p id=mwBME>A 2007 study by researchers from <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dartmouth_College title="Dartmouth College" id=mwBMI>Dartmouth College</a> found that "anonymous and infrequent contributors to Wikipedia<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwBMM>&nbsp;</span>... are as reliable a source of knowledge as those contributors who register with the site".<sup about=#mwt566 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-sciam_good_samaritans_1_179-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"sciam good samaritans 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-sciam_good_samaritans_1-179"}}'><a href=#cite_note-sciam_good_samaritans_1-179 id=mwBMQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBMU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBMY>[</span>170<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBMc>]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jimmy_Wales title="Jimmy Wales" id=mwBMg>Jimmy Wales</a> stated in 2009 that "[I]t turns out over 50% of all the edits are done by just 0.7% of the users<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwBMk>&nbsp;</span>... 524 people<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwBMo>&nbsp;</span>... And in fact, the most active 2%, which is 1400 people, have done 73.4% of all the edits."<sup about=#mwt567 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-blodget_173-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"blodget"}}'><a href=#cite_note-blodget-173 id=mwBMs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBMw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBM0>[</span>164<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBM4>]</span></span></a></sup> However, <i id=mwBM8><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business_Insider title="Business Insider" id=mwBNA>Business Insider</a></i> editor and journalist <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Blodget title="Henry Blodget" id=mwBNE>Henry Blodget</a> showed in 2009 that in a random sample of articles, most Wikipedia content (measured by the amount of contributed text that survives to the latest sampled edit) is created by "outsiders", while most editing and formatting is done by "insiders".<sup about=#mwt568 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-blodget_173-2 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"blodget"}}'><a href=#cite_note-blodget-173 id=mwBNI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBNM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBNQ>[</span>164<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBNU>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBNY>In 2008, a <i id=mwBNc>Slate</i> magazine article reported that "one percent of Wikipedia users are responsible for about half of the site's edits."<sup about=#mwt571 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-180 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-180"}}'><a href=#cite_note-180 id=mwBNg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBNk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBNo>[</span>171<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBNs>]</span></span></a></sup> This method of evaluating contributions was later disputed by <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aaron_Swartz title="Aaron Swartz" id=mwBNw>Aaron Swartz</a>, who noted that several articles he sampled had large portions of their content (measured by number of characters) contributed by users with low edit counts.<sup about=#mwt574 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-181 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-181"}}'><a href=#cite_note-181 id=mwBN0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBN4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBN8>[</span>172<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOA>]</span></span></a></sup> A 2008 study found that Wikipedians were less agreeable, open, and conscientious than others,<sup about=#mwt577 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-liebertonline_view_on_WP_users_1_182-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"liebertonline view on WP users 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-liebertonline_view_on_WP_users_1-182"}}'><a href=#cite_note-liebertonline_view_on_WP_users_1-182 id=mwBOE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBOI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOM>[</span>173<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOQ>]</span></span></a></sup> although a later commentary pointed out serious flaws, including that the data showed higher openness and that the differences with the control group and the samples were small.<sup about=#mwt580 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-183 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-183"}}'><a href=#cite_note-183 id=mwBOU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBOY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOc>[</span>174<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOg>]</span></span></a></sup> According to a 2009 study, there is "evidence of growing resistance from the Wikipedia community to new content".<sup about=#mwt583 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-newscientist_WP_boom_to_bust_1_184-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"newscientist WP boom to bust 1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-newscientist_WP_boom_to_bust_1-184"}}'><a href=#cite_note-newscientist_WP_boom_to_bust_1-184 id=mwBOk><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBOo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOs>[</span>175<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOw>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section><section data-mw-section-id=20 id=mwBO0 aria-labelledby=Diversity><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=Diversity>Diversity</h3></div>
<p id=mwBO4>Several studies have shown that most volunteer Wikipedia contributors are male. The results of a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikimedia_Foundation title="Wikimedia Foundation" id=mwBO8>Wikimedia Foundation</a> survey in 2008 showed that only 13<span typeof=mw:Entity id=mwBPA>&nbsp;</span>percent of Wikipedia editors were female.<sup about=#mwt586 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-185 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-185"}}'><a href=#cite_note-185 id=mwBPE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBPI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBPM>[</span>176<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBPQ>]</span></span></a></sup> Because of this, universities throughout the United States tried to encourage women to become Wikipedia contributors.<sup about=#mwt587 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-CBC_News-2013_186-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"CBC News-2013"}}'><a href=#cite_note-CBC_News-2013-186 id=mwBPU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBPY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBPc>[</span>177<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBPg>]</span></span></a></sup> Similarly, many of these universities, including <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale_University title="Yale University" id=mwBPk>Yale</a> and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown_University title="Brown University" id=mwBPo>Brown</a>, gave college credit to students who create or edit an article relating to women in science or technology.<sup about=#mwt590 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-CBC_News-2013_186-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"CBC News-2013"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-CBC_News-2013-186"}}'><a href=#cite_note-CBC_News-2013-186 id=mwBPs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBPw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBP0>[</span>177<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBP4>]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Lih title="Andrew Lih" id=mwBP8>Andrew Lih</a>, a professor and scientist, said that the reason he thought the number of male contributors outnumbered the number of females so greatly was because identifying as a woman may expose oneself to "ugly, intimidating behavior".<sup about=#mwt593 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Bloomberg_2016_187-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Bloomberg 2016"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Bloomberg_2016-187"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Bloomberg_2016-187 id=mwBQA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBQE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBQI>[</span>178<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBQM>]</span></span></a></sup> Data has shown that Africans are underrepresented among Wikipedia editors.<sup about=#mwt596 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-memeb_188-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"memeb"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-memeb-188"}}'><a href=#cite_note-memeb-188 id=mwBQQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBQU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBQY>[</span>179<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBQc>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
